Karl,
I checked and there are often not markers between the two inferred QTL.  I ran effect plot for one of the pairs of markers and this is what is shows:

R/qtl tells me that there are two additive QTLs at 8.6 cM and 12.6 cM on this chromosome.  c12m6 is at 8.3 cM and c12M10 is at 17.53 cM.  There are no markers in between them, they are just mislabeled.  I am not sure what is going on here.

-Emma

On Tuesday, June 19, 2012 10:12:41 AM UTC-5, Karl Broman wrote:

> "I will get two additive markers that are only 2 cM apart.  I am assuming this is because of the problems with marker order.  Is there any way to fix this or should I just do ANOVA qtl mapping?"
>
> You want to first correct problems in marker order if possible.  Are there markers in-between those two inferred QTL?  I would use effectplot() or plotPXG() to plot the genotypes at the two loci against the phenotype.  Often a few outlying phenotypes will lead to inference of two QTL linked tightly in repulsion.
>
> karl
>
>
>
> On Jun 18, 2012, at 11:22 AM, emma wrote:
>
> > I should also mention that I am mapping the traits in hybrid individuals.
> >
> >
> > On Sunday, June 17, 2012 7:56:31 PM UTC-5, emma wrote:
> > Hello,
> > I am trying to map several species specific traits but I am having trouble because marker order is poorly conserved between species.  I using one of the two linkage maps to do the QTL mapping but I keep getting odd results.  For example I will get two additive markers that are only 2 cM apart.  I am assuming this is because of the problems with marker order.  Is there any way to fix this or should I just do ANOVA qtl mapping?
> >
> > Any help would be greatly greatly appreciated,
> > Emma
> >
> >
